 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Overview",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A 60 V, 600 mA step-down converter with an integrated high-side MOSFET and an external catch diode. Peak current mode control at a fixed 2.1 MHz; soft-start and loop compensation are internal. The boot recharge diode is integrated. The output can be set as low as the reference voltage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Control loop",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Each cycle the oscillator turns the high-side switch on; an error amplifier compares FB with the internal reference and drives an internal COMP node, and the switch turns off when its current reaches the level COMP sets. COMP rises and falls with load current. Clamping COMP at a maximum level gives the cycle-by-cycle current limit."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bootstrap and high duty cycle",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "A small ceramic capacitor from CB to SW supplies the high-side gate drive; it recharges while the switch is off and the diode conducts. The part runs at up to 97% duty as long as CB to SW stays above 3 V. Below 3 V a UVLO turns the switch off so the diode conducts and the capacitor refreshes. Because the CB load is small the switch can stay on for many cycles between refreshes, so the effective duty cycle stays high. At light load or in dropout an internal circuit charges the CB capacitor so SW is pulled low for refresh."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Enable (SHDN) and undervoltage lockout",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "SHDN tolerates high voltage and has an internal pull-up, so a floating pin enables the part. Logic of 1.23 V or more turns it on. Driving SHDN from a higher voltage calls for a series resistor of 100 kΩ or more; without shutdown control, SHDN can go to VIN through 100 kΩ. SHDN should not exceed 60 V. At 0 V on SHDN the part shuts down and draws about 1 µA. An internal UVLO holds the output off below a fixed input threshold; a resistor divider on SHDN sets a higher system UVLO."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current limit",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Switch current is compared with COMP every cycle and the switch is turned off where they meet. In an overload that pulls the output down, the error amplifier drives COMP up until its internal clamp, which sets the peak switch current limit (1200 mA typical)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Overvoltage transient protection",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "After an overload or a fast load release, the error amplifier can lag the output and cause overshoot, most of all with small output capacitance. A comparator holds the high-side switch off while FB is above 108% of the reference, and lets it turn on again at the next clock once FB drops back below that level."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal shutdown",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Switching stops when the junction exceeds 170 °C (typical) and the part restarts its power-up sequence once the junction falls below 160 °C (typical)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Continuous conduction mode",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "With inductor current always above zero, each period has two phases: switch on with the diode reverse biased, the inductor charging and supplying the load with COUT; then switch off with the diode forward biased, the inductor discharging into COUT and the load. How long each phase lasts relative to the other fixes VOUT."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ECO mode",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "At light load the part saves switching and gate drive loss: when VOUT is regulated and a cycle ends with peak switch current under about 80 mA, it enters ECO mode and the input quiescent current falls to 28 µA. Entry depends on peak current, so the load at which it happens varies with VIN, VOUT and the inductor."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inductor selection",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Inductance, saturation current and DC resistance matter most. Size the inductor at the highest input, where ripple is largest, for ripple of 30% to 40% of output current. Neither the RMS nor the saturation rating may be exceeded. Lower ripple lowers output ripple but needs more inductance. A 6.8 µH part rated 1.6 A suits most designs; at that rating the current limit acts before the core saturates, rather than relying on thermal shutdown in a short or long overload."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output capacitor selection",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "COUT is set by the largest of three needs: holding droop during a load step for about two clock cycles (Equation 5), absorbing inductor energy on load release (Equation 6), and output ripple (Equations 7 and 8). Ceramic ESR can be ignored in Equation 5; electrolytic and tantalum ESR cannot. Derate for ageing, temperature and DC bias. 4.7 µF to 100 µF with ESR of 0.7 Ω or less is a good start; the example uses 10 µF ceramic."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Schottky diode selection",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Breakdown rating 25% above the highest input voltage; average current rating at least the maximum output current. With VIN close to VOUT the diode averages only about (1 - D) × IOUT, so a smaller average rating can do, but its peak rating must still be above the highest load current. Start from a part rated 0.5 A to 1 A."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input capacitor selection",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "A low-ESR ceramic, 1 µF to 10 µF, X5R or X7R, between VIN and GND. Ceramic capacitance can drop by up to half at rated voltage. Its ripple current rating must exceed the input ripple current (Equation 9). The example uses one 2.2 µF, 50 V part: 33 mV input ripple and 0.3 A rms."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bootstrap capacitor selection",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "0.1 µF ceramic or larger; 0.1 µF to 1 µF where VIN is close to VOUT, to keep gate drive up and RDS(on) low. X7R or X5R, rated 10 V or more."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input supply",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "The supply must deliver the peak input current while staying above 4 V; a current step through its source resistance must not pull VIN low enough to trip UVLO falsely. A distant supply (several inches or more) may need bulk capacitance besides the ceramic input capacitors."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Keep R1 and R2 near FB and away from the inductor. Place CIN close to VIN. Place L1 close to SW. Place COUT close to the L1-D1 junction and keep the L1, D1 and COUT loop short. Keep the ground connections of D1, CIN and COUT small and join them to the system ground plane at one point, preferably at the COUT ground. AN-1149 (SNVA021) covers switching supply layout further."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Grades",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "AEC-Q100 Grade 1 automotive part, -40 °C to +125 °C operating junction temperature."
  }
 ],
